# This project installs with yarn 1 (see `packageManager` in package.json);
# yarn.lock is the source of truth. This file only stops `npm install` from
# failing in a way that leaves a half-built node_modules behind.
#
# npm 7+ treats an unsatisfied peer dependency as a hard ERESOLVE error and
# aborts. When it does, packages that ARE correctly declared (e.g.
# @react-native-community/netinfo, jest-environment-jsdom) never get installed,
# and their test suites later fail with "Cannot find module" — which reads like
# a missing dependency rather than the failed install it actually was.
#
# The one conflict that was OUR bug — @react-navigation/native pinned to 7.0.19
# while @react-navigation/bottom-tabs floated to a version needing a newer peer
# — is fixed: those three packages are now pinned to a matched set and yarn no
# longer warns about them.
#
# What remains is upstream peer ranges that have not kept up, and cannot be
# fixed from this repo:
#   • @rneui/themed@4.0.0-rc.8 declares peer @rneui/base@4.0.0-rc.7 EXACTLY —
#     an RC packaging bug where it pins to the previous RC of itself. Escaping
#     it means bumping @rneui to 5.0.0, a major version of the UI component
#     library used across Settings; that needs its own ticket and visual QA.
#   • react-localization declares react ^18 max; this app runs react 19.
#   • reduxjs-toolkit-persist declares @reduxjs/toolkit ^1.6.1; this app uses 2.x.
#   • eslint-config-airbnb declares eslint-plugin-react-hooks ^4.3.0.
#
# All four are warnings under yarn and the app ships fine on them. This flag
# makes npm resolve peers the same way, so an npm user gets a complete
# node_modules instead of a confusing partial one.
#
# NOTE: there is no package-lock.json, so `npm install` still resolves
# transitive versions independently of yarn.lock. Yarn remains the only
# reproducible install; this is a guardrail, not an endorsement of npm.
legacy-peer-deps=true
